  "articleBody": "Painting a bathroom requires more than just choosing a color swatch and rolling on fresh paint. Bathrooms experience intense daily humidity, rapid temperature spikes, and harsh cleaning chemicals that cause standard wall coatings to bubble, peel, or foster persistent mildew. If your existing paint is already flaking around the shower or vanity mirror, simply applying a new topcoat will fail within months.\n\nA durable bathroom paint job starts with identifying and correcting moisture issues. First, check your mechanical exhaust fan to make sure it vents damp air outside effectively. Next, wash away soap scum and surface mildew using appropriate cleaning solutions, and scrape away all loose, flaking layers. Bare drywall patches, repaired water stains, and glossy surfaces demand an adhesion-promoting, moisture-resistant primer before any color goes on.\n\nWhat to Expect During the Process:\nPlan for thorough surface prep, patching, and sanding before painting begins. Following EPA recommendations, keep exhaust fans running during painting and maintain continuous ventilation for two to three days afterward to allow the coating to cure properly. In homes built before 1978, always address potential lead-based coatings safely before disturbing old layers. If your walls have damaged drywall or recurrent water stains, professional refinishing is your best bet. Call HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 to plan your bathroom painting project with Abraham Becerra.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
